<DocsLayout
  title="History"
  subtitle="Every project is a git repository. SQLite is the working copy. GitHub is an optional mirror."
  description="CADCAMFUN git-style history: objects, branches, pull requests, and what is still pending."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      WP11 on the task board is <strong>done</strong> for core, sidecar, and API. The same
      sentence says desktop panels and GitHub routes are pending. Owner directory:
      <code>packages/vcs</code> (<code>@cadcamfun/vcs</code>), plus the
      <code>GitRepo</code>, <code>GitObject</code>, <code>GitRef</code>, and
      <code>PullRequest*</code> tables, and the sidecar <code>vcs</code> services. Spec:
      <code>specs/13-vcs-actions-mobile.md</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="The history route exists. The graph does not.">
      <p>
        <code>/projects/:id/history</code> is in <code>apps/desktop/src/app/routes.tsx</code>.
        Spec 13 §8 says that Version control screen and the per-item History buttons are built.
        A drawn commit graph and in-editor CAD/CAM history panels are not. GitHub pull-request
        mirroring is not built. <code>PullRequest.remoteNumber</code> is reserved. A live check
        that GitHub’s Git Data API reproduces an oid is not done. Unit tests prove the hash.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="model" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Model</h2>
    <p>
      Blobs, trees, and commits use git’s loose-object format and SHA-1. An oid matches
      <code>git cat-file</code> and, when the mirror works, GitHub. Tests in
      <code>packages/vcs/test</code> check that against the real <code>git</code> binary. SQLite
      holds the checked-out working copy. <code>commit</code> snapshots it into the repository.
      <code>checkout</code>, <code>merge</code>, and <code>restore</code> write a tree back. A
      dirty working copy blocks checkout, merge, and restore.
    </p>
    <p>
      Collaboration is internal. Access follows project ownership and organization roles.
      Members read, commit to branches, and open and review pull requests. The owner or an
      organization admin or manager moves shared branches and merges. <code>GitRepo.remote</code>
      (<code>owner/repo</code>) is the optional GitHub link. Legacy <code>*Version</code> tables
      stay for the old routes. They are not this history.
    </p>
    <p>
      An AI actor cannot commit, restore, or merge on <code>main</code>. The phone’s assistant
      commits to <code>ai/*</code> branches and opens a pull request. A person merges. A review
      of <code>request-changes</code> (latest verdict per reviewer) blocks the merge. Conflicts
      leave the repository untouched and return the conflicting paths and JSON pointers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="package" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Package</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/vcs</code> is pure aside from <code>node:crypto</code>. It does not open
      SQLite. The sidecar <code>ProjectVcsRepo</code> implements the <code>Backend</code>
      interface on the git tables.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>File</th><th>Role</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>src/object.ts</code></td><td>Loose git objects and SHA-1 oids.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/store.ts</code></td><td><code>Backend</code> interface and the memory backend.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/repository.ts</code></td><td>Commit, log, branch, tag, checkout, merge (fast-forward, 3-way, <code>mergeInto</code>), <code>revertTo</code>, diff.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/json.ts</code></td><td>Canonical JSON and <code>mergeJson</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/diff.ts</code></td><td><code>describeDiff</code>: entity, property pointer, before and after.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/snapshot.ts</code></td><td>Project rows to files and back.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/github.ts</code></td><td><code>push</code>, <code>fetchBranch</code>, <code>pull</code> over a <code>GitHubTransport</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/transport.ts</code></td><td><code>restTransport</code> for the Git Data API. Tested against an in-memory remote that enforces fast-forward and checks every object hash.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Sidecar pieces: <code>apps/sidecar/src/repos/project-vcs-repo.ts</code>,
      <code>apps/sidecar/src/services/project-vcs.ts</code>,
      <code>apps/sidecar/src/http/vcs.ts</code>, and
      <code>apps/sidecar/src/services/ai-vcs-tools.ts</code>. The API group is
      <code>packages/api/src/groups/vcs.ts</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tree" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Tree</h2>
    <p>
      Canonical JSON (sorted keys, two-space indent) keeps text diffs stable. Two people editing
      different properties of one drawing do not conflict. The same property, or any array,
      conflicts with a JSON pointer. Applying a commit creates missing library rows and updates
      an existing one only when the current user owns it. Owners and organizations are
      installation-specific and are not stored in the tree.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="text" title="Tracked paths (spec 13 §2)">
{`project.json
drawings/<id>.json
components/<id>.json
toolpaths/<id>.json
materials/<id>.json
tools/<id>.json
machine-configs/<id>.json
costs/<id>.json
.cadcamfun/workflows/<name>.yml`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      Toolpath files include generated G-code. Not tracked: thumbnails, comments, chat, uploaded
      binaries, and per-user cost settings. Every history-changing action is also appended to
      <code>ActivityLog</code>. <code>GET …/vcs/log?path=drawings/&lt;id&gt;.json</code> lists
      commits that touched that file. <code>POST …/vcs/restore</code> with <code>paths</code>
      restores only those files as a new commit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>log</code> is linear in history length. <code>isAncestor</code> walks the graph.
      Spec 13 says that is fine for thousands of commits. Packfile and delta compression are not
      implemented. Large STL and DXF blobs are out of this tree.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="http" class="doc-section">
    <h2>HTTP</h2>
    <p>
      Routes are under <code>/api/projects/:id</code>. A commit or merge triggers
      <code>push</code> workflows for that branch. Opening a pull request triggers
      <code>pullRequest</code> workflows. A trigger failure does not fail the user’s operation.
      The API accepts non-model files only under <code>.cadcamfun/workflows/*.yml</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Route</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>GET …/vcs/status</code>, <code>/log</code>, <code>/branches</code>, <code>/diff?from&amp;to</code></td><td>Read state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST …/vcs/commit</code>, <code>/branches</code>, <code>/checkout</code>, <code>/restore</code></td><td>Write state. Commit takes <code>message</code> and optional workflow <code>files</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>GET/PUT …/vcs/remote</code>, <code>POST …/vcs/push</code>, <code>/pull</code></td><td>GitHub mirror. Header <code>x-github-token</code>. Desktop only.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>GET/POST …/pulls</code>, reviews, <code>/merge</code>, <code>/close</code></td><td>Pull requests.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>GET …/actions/runs</code>, <code>POST …/actions/dispatch</code></td><td>Workflow runs. See <a href="/docs/workflows">Workflows</a>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The companion gateway allow-list includes project, vcs, pulls, and actions reads, and the
      AI chat routes. It refuses <code>/vcs/push</code> and <code>/vcs/pull</code>. The phone
      never holds the desktop bearer token or the GitHub token. The desktop keeps the GitHub
      token in the OS keychain and sends it per request. The sidecar does not store it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Phone flow: <code>vcs_status</code>, <code>vcs_start_change</code> (creates
      <code>ai/&lt;topic&gt;</code> and refuses a dirty working copy), CAD or CAM edits,
      <code>vcs_commit</code>, <code>vcs_open_pull_request</code>. After a person merges, the
      desktop working copy updates when <code>main</code> is checked out, and a configured
      remote is pushed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="github" class="doc-section">
    <h2>GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      <code>restTransport</code> speaks the Git Data API with a fine-grained token.
      Fast-forward-only updates are what the in-memory test remote enforces. The HTTP routes
      exist. What spec 13 §7 marks undone: mirroring CADCAMFUN pull requests onto GitHub pull
      requests, and a live round-trip that proves GitHub stores the same commit oid for a given
      author and committer date. Treat the mirror as implemented against the transport tests,
      and treat production GitHub behaviour as unverified.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Boundaries</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>packages/vcs</code> must not open SQLite. The sidecar backend does.</li>
      <li>Checkout, merge, and restore must refuse a dirty working copy.</li>
      <li>AI tools must not commit or merge on <code>main</code>.</li>
      <li>The companion gateway must not forward GitHub push, pull, credentials, or plugin routes.</li>
      <li>Thumbnails, chat, and binaries must not be committed into the tree.</li>
      <li>Do not hand-edit the git table migration. It was generated from <code>packages/db/src/schema.ts</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
